WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.046 --> 00:00:10.030
One banana for breakfast, 90 calories.

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:17.020
Lettuce salad and half of piece
of bread for lunch, 300 calories.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:22.749
Cucumber salad for dinner,

00:00:22.749 --> 00:00:28.195
80 calories, total, 470 calories.

00:00:28.195 --> 00:00:31.710
That was it for the day.

00:00:34.754 --> 00:00:38.040
I spent the most of my middle school
dealing with an eating disorder.

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:43.000
It started from an article I
read about tracking your eating.

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:49.810
Write down everything you've eaten, and
the guilt it creates induces you to

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:54.904
eat less, and less, and less.

00:00:54.904 --> 00:01:01.499
Every week I lost a pound, and
this lasted for about six months.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.250
It started during my second year
at a boarding school in the UK.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:13.880
I was a child with
an extremely low self-esteem.

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:18.400
If you were to meet me then, you would
have seen the smallest girl in the class,

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:23.605
who couldn't speak fluent English,
always hanging out by herself, or

00:01:23.605 --> 00:01:24.650
with a very few friends.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.600
Accomplishing my goals and
diet gave me confidence and

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.930
hope, that I could become my
ideal self with enough effort.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.840
Living in a boarding school helped
me hide my diet from everyone.

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:43.630
The Boarding Mistress was taking
attendance outside of the canteen every

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:46.250
day, to make sure we all ate.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:51.170
So I showed up for the attendance, picked
up an apple, and went back to my room.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:56.580
I enjoyed being in full
control of my weight.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:04.090
I didn't realize I was entering
a bottomless swamp until it was too late.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:11.210
I still remember the fear I felt
when I saw the shower drain

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:14.420
filled with my own hair,
that had started to fall off excessively.

00:02:16.298 --> 00:02:21.460
I didn't know, then, that the interruption
in my menstrual cycle was a bigger issue.

00:02:23.902 --> 00:02:26.770
I didn't know how to let go of my diet.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:33.200
And my family's decision was to take care
of me at home, instead of using therapies.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:38.960
The mere idea of having to ask for
help made me feel ashamed.

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:43.340
My family was horrified, though.

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:47.150
My mother made every sacrifice and effort.

00:02:48.418 --> 00:02:52.390
She even flew back to Japan with me,
so that I could focus on studies,

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:55.340
friends, anything other
than restricting food.

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:59.740
We suffered through without
sharing it with anyone.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.930
Research by Stanford Medical School shows
that with only six months of family

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:11.780
therapies, 80% of adolescence
anorexia can be reduced.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:18.550
For me, it took three years to
recover fully, but I was still lucky.

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:24.540
Adolescence anorexia can lead to
problems in hormone imbalance,

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:29.470
bone development, heart disease, brain
damage, and many other complications.

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:34.140
40% of anorexia are chronic.

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:36.190
They come back again.

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:41.345
And suicide attempts after
anorexia can be up to 20%.

00:03:44.017 --> 00:03:48.786
As I spoke with my Stanford classmates,
who have gone through similar experience,

00:03:48.786 --> 00:03:51.608
and successfully recovered
through therapies,

00:03:51.608 --> 00:03:55.590
I started to realize that it doesn't
necessarily have to be this way.

00:03:56.930 --> 00:04:02.230
And I started wondering what was causing
the shame, the shame that I was feeling.

00:04:05.541 --> 00:04:08.644
Jan Si,
at Stanford Neuroscience Institute, and

00:04:08.644 --> 00:04:13.264
many other psychologists define shame
as a self conscious feeling we feel,

00:04:13.264 --> 00:04:16.310
when we think we have done
something bad or wrong.

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:21.660
It is similar to guilt in
the way that it's generated,

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:24.839
when we don't meet our standards or norms.

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:31.440
Whether we care more about how other
people see us and feel ashamed, or

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.110
we care more about how we
feel about ourselves and

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:36.930
feel guilt,
is dependent on culture context.

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:39.610
But the root cause is the same.

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:43.510
We think we have done something bad or
wrong.

00:04:45.429 --> 00:04:49.669
And for some of us,
the mere idea of going to a therapy and

00:04:49.669 --> 00:04:53.020
seeking help causes this feeling of shame.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:57.840
Seeking help is bad because
it's a show of weakness.

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:04.740
But, would you consider someone
going to a gym as physically weak?

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:11.370
We have no problem getting a trainer, and
a self-assessment, and planning a workout.

00:05:13.002 --> 00:05:17.439
So how is improving their mental
health through therapies so

00:05:17.439 --> 00:05:22.140
different from improving their
physical health through a gym?

00:05:25.167 --> 00:05:28.886
I witnessed a similar sense of shame
at my first apartment in Tokyo.

00:05:31.276 --> 00:05:35.746
I heard this strange knocking noise
[SOUND] from the floor above,

00:05:35.746 --> 00:05:37.830
every night from around ten.

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:42.265
[SOUND] At first,
I thought the family above us was using

00:05:42.265 --> 00:05:46.615
some sort of fitness machine,
and made a complaint.

00:05:46.615 --> 00:05:51.808
[SOUND] The noise went away for
a few days, but it came back again.

00:05:51.808 --> 00:05:56.568
[SOUND] So one night,
I decided to go up and

00:05:56.568 --> 00:06:00.069
confront the family myself.

00:06:00.069 --> 00:06:01.380
I was getting really annoyed.

00:06:03.568 --> 00:06:05.565
As I approached their entrance,

00:06:05.565 --> 00:06:09.280
I heard this girl's weeping
voice leaking out of their door.

00:06:11.350 --> 00:06:15.643
I still knocked, and I realized
that the source of the noise was

00:06:15.643 --> 00:06:19.290
their oldest girl hitting
on their living room wall.

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:24.420
She had develop a symptom of autism

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:27.210
a year into the top rank
elementary school in Japan.

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:33.650
The family was trying to take care of the
daughter themselves, just like my family.

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:37.540
I've had problems, too.

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:39.960
I think you can ask for help.

00:06:42.122 --> 00:06:45.480
How I wish I could have
said that on the spot.

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:50.265
Instead, all I did was,

00:06:50.265 --> 00:06:54.790
I said that I acknowledged the situation,
and walked off.

00:06:56.170 --> 00:06:58.460
And to this date, it is my biggest regret.

00:07:02.278 --> 00:07:05.107
I wonder how much pain
I could have removed,

00:07:05.107 --> 00:07:08.160
if I could tell them about my own story.

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:11.980
And make them realize that their problems
didn't mean there was something uniquely

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:12.600
wrong with them.

00:07:14.730 --> 00:07:18.020
I wonder how supportive I could have been,

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:21.790
if I could explain to them that seeking
help is nothing to be ashamed of.

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:27.920
Do you remember your first day at the GSP?

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:33.750
We were told that nearly half
of the MBA students go and

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:36.370
visit the on campus therapy
during their two years.

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:42.270
This surprised me, and it comforted me.

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:47.600
If I have a problem again,
I can ask for help.

00:07:50.531 --> 00:07:55.397
But I know how hard it is,
as you're entering that bottomless swamp,

00:07:55.397 --> 00:07:57.880
to take step back and seek for help.

00:07:59.785 --> 00:08:04.422
This is why I want you to be there,
open and supportive, when your family and

00:08:04.422 --> 00:08:06.170
friends are in need of help.

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:12.110
If you're having an experience
in dealing with mental issues,

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:15.010
I want you to share your story
with your family and friends.

00:08:16.641 --> 00:08:20.320
Sharing can remove the stigma
associated with disclosure.

00:08:23.788 --> 00:08:26.240
I want you to learn about
the services around you.

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:31.050
If you're a Stanford student,
you can use the on campus therapy for

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:35.068
free, because it's already covered by
the campus health fee we're all paying.

00:08:35.068 --> 00:08:41.830
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; At Stanford, it is seen okay,

00:08:41.830 --> 00:08:46.129
or it's even encouraged to seek for
help when we're having problems.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.900
But in many other places,
this is not the case.

00:08:53.423 --> 00:08:55.150
Look back at your own community.

00:08:56.570 --> 00:08:59.610
Was your family, your friends, or

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:04.550
even your employer, open to the idea
of seeking help for mental issues?

00:09:06.177 --> 00:09:10.027
If not, I want you to join me
in becoming the first supporter

00:09:10.027 --> 00:09:12.340
of seeking help in your community.

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:19.048
I want you to open the people's
mind to the fact that seeking help

00:09:19.048 --> 00:09:23.255
is nothing special,
it's just like going to the gym.

00:09:23.255 --> 00:09:24.211
Thank you.

00:09:24.211 --> 00:09:27.214
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:27.214 --> 00:09:31.193
[MUSIC]

